James Of Tucson <james0tucson@gmail.com> writes:

> My idea is simply:  Avoid putting wands into bags.    It follows:  If
> you put wands into bags you increase the chances of putting that bag
> into another bag.  Careful play means stuff like careful #adjustment

I put other wands in bags, and might put cancellation in other bags, but I
just don't put wands of cancellation in bags of holding.

To be sure, that includes:
- I never put a wand that MIGHT be non-empty cancellation (totally
unidentified or engrave-identified as one that makes the engraving vanish)
in a bag of holding or a bag that COULD be holding (not known to be some
other bag) 
- I never put a could-be-holding bag to another could-be-holding bag:
whenever I pick up a bag of unknown type, I non-individually #name it ("1"
for the first one, "2" for the first one that is not "1"..) and don't put
a bag into another one of the same type
- And I never put any other bag in a bag of holding

That way,
- I DO have non-cancellation wands or possibly empty wands of cancellation
in bags, but it doesn't matter
- I may have wands of cancellation in sacks or oilskin bags, but it
doesn't matter
- I may put a bag of holding into a sack or oilskin bag, but it doesn't
matter

It makes little sense to put a sack or an oilskin bag in a a bag of
holding. When you put one bag into another, it's better to put holding in
non-holding than the other way round.
Whatever is in the other bag, if you want to reduce its weight, why not just
put it straight in the bag of holding instead of nesting it in the other
bag?
But you may want to put a bag of holding into an oilskin bag to protect
the precious contents from water, or the bag of holding from getting
cursed. 

All this is just a matter of discipline, and of course, if you feel that
you may make mistakes with my ruleset, never putting wands in bags is a more
foolproof single rule than following my ruleset.

> of inventory letters, careful naming of things like "***BOH*** BE
> CAREFUL", and generally making it impossible (or unlikely) to happen

This kind of naming reduces mistakes, which is good.

